<VsLayout slug="fluently">
  <p>Fluently costs $24.99 a month on the US App Store, and annual plans run from $89.99 to $179.99. It isn't free: the level assessment and some practice games are, but conversations with the AI tutor need Premium. Fluently says Premium includes 100 speaking minutes a week, and lifting the cap takes a separate Pro plan.</p>

  <p>I checked all of this on 26 November 2026 against Fluently's App Store and Google Play listings, its own site and its replies to Play reviews. I build Vocele, a competing speaking app, so below you'll also find what Fluently does better than Vocele, and where Vocele is the better fit.</p>

  <h2>Fluently price in 2026</h2>

  <p>The <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fluently-ai-language-learning/id6683289805">US App Store listing</a> shows these in-app purchases:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Plan</th><th>US App Store price</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Premium, monthly</td><td>$24.99</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Premium, 3 months</td><td>$44.99 or $89.99</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Premium, annual</td><td>$89.99 or $179.99</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Premium, weekly</td><td>$9.99 or $19.99</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Workplace English course</td><td>$19.99, sold separately</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Paid yearly, the $89.99 plan works out to about $7.50 a month; paid weekly, $9.99 comes to roughly $43 a month.</p>

  <p>The listing names every subscription "Premium", so it doesn't say which of the two prices per period buys Pro. Fluently has no public pricing page. The price appears on a paywall after the sign-up questions, and Fluently's help centre says prices can vary by country or region. Its FAQ also pitches the app as "a human tutor, but available 24/7 and 20x cheaper."</p>

  <h2>Is Fluently free?</h2>

  <p>No, beyond a first look. Its <a href="https://getfluently.app/">FAQ</a> says you can start with a free AI speaking assessment and practice games, and that conversations with the tutor come with Premium. Fluently's replies on Google Play say the same: the assessment and its results are free, and full speaking practice needs a subscription, though one November reviewer said they couldn't see their results without paying. One November reply adds that "some offers include a 7-day trial, depending on availability".</p>

  <p>On refunds, a Fluently reply from November 2026 gives the rule: requests within 30 days for web purchases or 7 days on Google Play, provided you've spoken for less than 15 minutes. Buy through the App Store and the refund request goes to Apple.</p>

  <h2>Fluently's limit: 100 minutes a week</h2>

  <p>Premium has a cap. Fluently's own replies on Google Play, through November 2026, say "Premium includes 100 speaking minutes per week" and that Pro is an optional upgrade without that weekly cap. The minutes come back after the weekly reset, so hitting the cap doesn't force an upgrade, but it does stop the conversation until then.</p>

  <p>That works out at roughly 14 minutes a day. Several Play reviewers say they learned about the cap only by reaching it, one of them on the third day of the week. Fluently has replied to some of them that it appreciates the feedback about making the limit clearer before purchase.</p>

  <p>I take this seriously because I've been on the other side of it. Apple rejected Vocele three times under its subscription-disclosure rule before launch. I ended up writing a checker that fails our build when a paywall screen can't name the price a trial converts into, and it caught a "Start 3 days free" screen doing exactly that. So my advice for any of these apps: before you pay, find the plan you're buying, its minutes and the renewal date.</p>

  <h2>Fluently app review: what users say</h2>

  <p>The ratings are high everywhere I looked: 4.8 from 6,742 ratings on the App Store, 4.7 from 46.7K reviews and more than a million downloads on <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=app.getfluently.app">Google Play</a>, and 4.8 from 2,783 reviews on <a href="https://www.trustpilot.com/review/getfluently.app">Trustpilot</a>. Trustpilot's AI summary says reviewers love how natural the conversation feels.</p>

  <p>The critical reviews on Google Play repeat a few themes: the weekly cap, a paywall that appears after a long sign-up, extra subscriptions offered right after you buy, and refunds refused once you've passed 15 minutes of speaking. A three-star reviewer in November 2026 said those extra offers made the plan they'd bought feel "basic".</p>

  <h2>What Fluently does well</h2>

  <p>Fluently's loop is a voice conversation with an AI tutor. Its <a href="https://getfluently.app/about">About page</a> says that after your turn a card can show what to fix, from pronunciation "down to individual sounds" to how a native speaker would phrase the same thought, and that your level is scored across pronunciation, fluency, vocabulary, grammar and coherence.</p>

  <p>The homepage leans into work English: team meetings, presentations, negotiations ("Hold Your Ground and Close Deals") and interview practice. If your problem is sounding sharper at work, it's aimed squarely at you, and Fluently for Teams sells the same tutor to employers.</p>

  <p>It covers far more languages than it used to. The About page now says "Fluently teaches 22 languages", up from the 15 it listed when I checked on 2 November, with the app interface in English, Japanese, Portuguese, Spanish and French.</p>

  <p>Real-call feedback is where Fluently started. It began in 2023, per the same About page, as "a desktop AI speaking coach that listens to your side of real work calls". I couldn't find that feature on its current homepage or store listings, so I can't tell you whether you can buy it today.</p>

  <h2>Where Fluently falls short</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No way to try a conversation for free.</strong> The assessment is free; practice isn't, unless your offer includes a trial.</li>
    <li><strong>A weekly cap on the plan most people buy.</strong> 100 speaking minutes a week on Premium, with the cap lifted only on Pro.</li>
    <li><strong>A strict refund window.</strong> Over 15 minutes of speaking and Fluently's own refund rule no longer applies.</li>
    <li><strong>It assumes you can already talk.</strong> Its own FAQ says it's "built for non-native English speakers who already know the basics", and its scenarios are meetings and negotiations. If you understand everything and produce almost nothing, a five-axis score mostly tells you what you already know.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Vocele does differently</h2>

  <p>Start with the minuses. Vocele doesn't listen to or grade your real calls, and it has no professional scenario catalogue. It's iPhone only, and it isn't on the App Store yet. Those are real gaps if you're Fluently's target user.</p>

  <h3>Built for people who freeze</h3>

  <p>Vocele starts from the person who has a parent-teacher conference on Thursday and has dreaded it for a week. The coaches, Luna and Lorenzo, run in three modes: full scaffolding when you need help building the sentence, corrections-only when you just want to be fixed, and immersive when you want no interruptions. Conversations are open-topic, so you rehearse the conversation you're actually facing, and the next day's plan is built from what you got wrong.</p>

  <h3>Corrections you can see</h3>

  <p>Fluently shows a card after your turn. Vocele highlights the wrong words inside the bubble you just spoke, with the fix beneath. I chose that over a separate report because a report tells you what went wrong and the highlight tells you where, and where is what sticks.</p>

  <h3>Pronunciation with a history</h3>

  <p>Both apps go below the word: Fluently's About page describes feedback down to individual sounds, and Vocele scores every word and names the sound that dragged it down. The difference I'd point to is time. Vocele keeps each sound's score history over weeks, and I couldn't find anything on Fluently's site or listing about tracking a sound over time. I'd argue the trend matters more than any single score; the <a href="/blog/ai-accent-training">accent training explainer</a> covers why.</p>

  <h3>Vocele shows its minutes before you pay</h3>

  <p>Vocele meters spoken conversation and pronunciation scoring on every plan, and says how much before you pay. Core is $14.99 a month with 150 conversation minutes and 30 pronunciation minutes; Fluency ($29.99) and Meta ($49.99) double and quadruple that. Word sets, stories, reviews and grammar don't use minutes. The numbers are on the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> before you subscribe.</p>

  <h3>Saved words return on a schedule</h3>

  <p>Saved vocabulary comes back on a spaced-repetition schedule across six practice modes, and any phrase from a chat can go into a phrasebook with its own reviews. Vocele teaches Spanish, English, French, German, Italian, Portuguese, Japanese, Russian and Polish, with grammar rules and word meanings shown in your native language.</p>

  <h3>Games, with rules</h3>

  <p>Fluently's free tier includes practice games. Vocele has streaks, duels and friends-only leaderboards. We built confetti bursts and XP count-ups, then killed them as off-brand. There are no guilt notifications, no strangers on your leaderboard, and no punitive streak reset.</p>

